Of course, rectangular coordinates $(x, y)$ are related to polar coordinates $(r, \theta)$, satisfying $x=r \cos\theta$ and $y=r \sin\theta$, which further implies $r=x^2+y^2$, and $\tan\theta=\dfrac{y}{x} $.
Alternatively, there are formulas for converting from rectangular coordinates $(x,y)$ to polar coordinates $(r, \theta)$: we have $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and $\theta=\arctan\left( \dfrac{y}{x}\right)$.
We are to find rectangular coordinates of the point with polar coordinates $(r^2, 2\theta).$ That is such a point $(U,V)$ that $U=r^2\cos2\theta$ and $V=r^2\sin2\theta$.
The formulas for the rectangular coordinates of the point whose polar to the rectangular formulas provide us with these expressions, consider the double angle formulas $$\cos2\theta=2\cos^2\theta-1,$$ $$\sin2\theta=2\sin\theta\cos\theta,$$ since we can get these formulas in the form $$\cos2\theta=\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta,$$ by the substitution $u=\cos\theta$, $v=\sin\theta$.
Alternatively, one can find this relations by using in the formulas which provide us with the expressions the formulas $$\cos\theta=\dfrac{x}{r} \quad \text{and} \quad \sin\theta=\dfrac{y}{r},$$ which reduce our case if we substitute $r^2=x^2+y^2$, which may be derived from rectangular to polar formulas above, and $\cos 2\theta=\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta$, which is a consequence of the formula for $\cos 2\theta$ above.
Moreover, by purely analogical substitution from sinus to cosine and vice versa, we get that $\sin2\theta=2\sin\theta\cos\theta=2\cos\theta\sin\theta $.
In conclusion, we get that the desired rectangular coordinates are $$\left( (x^2+y^2)(2\cos^2\theta-1), (x^2+y^2)(2\sin\theta\cos\theta) \right).$$
