Let us calculate the sum for a row, $S$. We have that:
\begin{align*} S=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{a}{p+n-1} \end{align*}

To simplify the sum, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by $(p+n-1)$, we get:
\begin{align*} S=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{a(p+n-1)}{(p+n-1)(p+n-1)} \end{align*}

We can simplify the denominator and rewrite the equation as:
\begin{align*} S=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{a(p+n-1)}{(p-1)^2+n^2} \end{align*}

This is a well-known series, it is the Riemann zeta function with argument $2$ and $a$ times a constant. The sum of an AR(1) series is $\frac{S}{1-\frac{1}{p}}$.

Rewriting $S$ with the sum (the denominator looks nicer):
\begin{align*} S= a\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{(p+n-1)}{p^2} =(a\frac{p}{p-1})\frac{S}{1-\frac{1}{p}} \end{align*}

This looks nice:
\begin{align*}(p-1)\frac{1}{p} \end{align*}

We can write the solution for an arbitrary row $n$ as
\begin{align*}\frac{m_n}{p \cdot n^2}, \end{align*}
where m_n is an arbitrary integer. Writing the full $\frac{1}{p}$-array with columns (taking radii afloat of the $\frac{1}{p^2}$ axis):
$$\bigg(
\begin{array}{c c c c c c}
1& & & & &\\
a\frac{p}{p-1}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& & & &\\
b\frac{p}{p-1}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& & &\\
c\frac{p}{p-1}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& \frac{mp}{p^2}& &\\
...
\end{array}
\bigg).$$
The sum of all the masses in the $\frac{1}{p}$ array is
$\large{\frac{S}{p(p-1)}}$ where $\frac{(a+b+c+...)}{(p-1)}$ is the strength of each mass in the array. Now take into account that this 3-dimensional structure lies on a surface everywhere else, so
$\large{\frac{S}{p(p-1)}}=\frac{1}{(p-1)^2}+\frac{1}{p(p-1)}= \frac{m}{n}$
so $\large{m+n} = \frac{(m+n)}{(p-1)^2} + \frac{1}{p(p-1)} =\frac{(m+n)(p+p-1)+(p-1)+1}{(p-1)p}$
